\f2\fs20 \cf5 \cb3 Hi\cb1 \
\cb3 Here are answers to the first four questions. I will get to the fifth, the list of 25 stories, tomorrow I hope.\'a0\cb1 \
\
\
\cb3 1. Mishnah stories. You told us a story inside a Mishnah belongs with Mishnah stories. In Gittin the same ma'aseh often appears twice \'97 once in the Mishnah and again where the Gemara quotes it back (10a/10b, 45b/46a, 74a/74b) \'97 so we catalogue one story twice. One entry or two, and which text would you cite?\cb1 \
\
\cb3 These cases should optimally go with the stories of the Mishnah, not the Talmud. That is, 10a would go with the stories in the Mishnah, and 10b, as you note, is the Talmud quoting the Mishnah, so it would not have to be counted separately.\'a0 Ideally, if we run the program on all of rabbinic literature, the story would be listed with the stories of the Mishnah, as Mishnah Gittin 1:5 with a parallel in bavli Gittin 10b. That said, I think for now the story that appears in the Talmud quoting the Mishnah (in this case 10b), could be listed with the talmudic stories from Gittin,\'a0 as it is a talmudic passage.\'a0 10a is just the Mishnah itself, that the printers included in the printing of the Talmud, but not technically part of the Talmud. So let's say for now the second instances\'a0(Gittin 10b, 46a 74b), where the Talmud quotes the story from the Mishnah, can be included.\'a0\'a0\cb1 \
\
\cb3 -2. Passages where only speech happens. You wrote in July that these are not stories. Your 2005 list includes them \'97 Gittin 38b (\uc0\u1488 \u1502 \u1512  \u1512 \u1489 \u1492 : \u1489 \u1492 \u1504 \u1497  \u1514 \u1500 \u1514  \u1502 \u1497 \u1500 \u1497 ), 57a (the exchange on the land's fertility), and 57a (Beitar, which opens with a custom). Three of our four misses are exactly this. In, out, or in with a borderline flag?\cb1 \
\
\cb3 This is a tricky question. Sometimes dialogue can be counted as stories (by some theorists at least) when there is conflict and implied change. But these would always be borderline. Unfortunately there are no real hard and fast rules, that is, unless we make one.\'a0 These lists, as I said, are provisional, so there may be some mistakes. Anyway, as to these cases:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 \'a0 \'a0(a) 57a, Beitar, is clearly a story. After the custom you have the one time event--- One day the emperor's daughter....\cb1 \
\
