\fs20 \cf5 \cb3 (b) 57a the exchange on fertility.\'a0 I agree that this is not a story and should not have been included. The list was wrong.\'a0 Great to have the AI correct it!\cb1 \
\
\cb3 (c) In 38b, the dialogue includes a story, in R. Yohanan's statement, that there were two families who set their meals at the wrong times and were uprooted. (two actions, causal connection)\cb1 \
\
\cb3 In connection with this issue,\'a0
\f3\b Rabba said: With these three matters homeowners become impoverished: That they emancipate their slaves; and that they inspect their property on Shabbat; and that they set their meals on Shabbat at the time of
\f2\b0 \'a0the sermon in\'a0
\f3\b the study hall,
\f2\b0 \'a0so that they miss it,\'a0
\f3\b as Rabbi \uc0\u7716 iyya bar Abba says
\f2\b0 \'a0that\'a0
\f3\b Rabbi Yo\uc0\u7717 anan says: There were two families in Jerusalem, one
\f2\b0 \'a0that\'a0
\f3\b set its meal on Shabbat and one
\f2\b0 \'a0that\'a0
\f3\b set its meal on the eve of Shabbat, and both of them were uprooted.
\f2\b0 \'a0One family was uprooted because they caused the suspension of Torah study, and the other was uprooted because by eating their meal on Shabbat eve, they did not properly distinguish between Shabbat and Shabbat eve.\cb1 \
\
\
\cb3 -3. The opening formula. Is \uc0\u1514 \u1504 \u1497 \u1488  / \u1488 \u1502 \u1512  \u1512 \u1489  \u1497 \u1492 \u1493 \u1491 \u1492  \u1488 \u1502 \u1512  \u1512 \u1489  part of the story you would publish, or the frame around it? We tried to settle it with a rule; it fixed nine boundaries and broke eight, because you yourself go both ways.\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Here the lists were sloppy and preliminary, and we had not worked this out. Basically it could go either way. These opening formulae are not technically\'a0part of the stories. But they are important, as, for example,\'a0\uc0\u1514 \u1504 \u1497 \u1488  indicates the Talmud thinks the story is Tannaitic. So ideally they should be included.\'a0 Likewise,\'a0\u1488 \u1502 \u1512  \u1512 \u1489  \u1497 \u1492 \u1493 \u1491 \u1492  \u1488 \u1502 \u1512  \u1512 \u1489 , attributes the story (perhaps mistakenly) to Rav Yehudah in the name of Rav, which might be significant to a scholar. If not too much trouble, we should include them.\'a0\cb1 \
\
\cb3 -4. Two passages we propose and you do not. Gittin 43a \'97 Rabba bar Rav Huna publicly retracts after Rav \uc0\u7716 isda challenges him; Gittin 25a \'97 \u1502 \u1506 \u1513 \u1492 , \u1493 \u1511 \u1491 \u1502 \u1493  \u1489 \u1504 \u1493 \u1514  \u1500 \u1489 \u1504 \u1497 \u1501 . Stories?\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Gittin 43a. Probably not a story, although could be "low confidence." It is really all speech except for Rav Huna "placing an interpreter", that is, a speaker, to broadcast his words. But that is the only action. However, there is some conflict and the retraction. So maybe "low confidence" but no more.\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Gittin 25a: Yes, this should be "high confidence." It is very terse but could be a story. (It depends a little on how it is understood. But should be included in our database for scholars to make their own assessment)\cb1 \
\
\cb3 All the best\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Jeff\cb1 \
